__d(function(e,i,t,n,o,a,r){o.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2014 Class 7 (NCERT)",chapter_number:9,chapter_title:"Rational Numbers",total_mcqs:20,mcqs:[{id:"mcq01",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following is a rational number?",options:["A) 5/0","B) \u221a2","C) \u22127/3","D) \u03c0"],correct_answer:"C) \u22127/3",explanation:"A rational number is p/q where p, q are integers and q \u2260 0. \u22127/3 satisfies this \u2014 p = \u22127, q = 3, both integers, q \u2260 0. 5/0 is undefined (q = 0). \u221a2 is irrational (cannot be written as p/q of integers). \u03c0 is irrational."},{id:"mcq02",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Is the integer \u221211 a rational number?",options:["A) No, negative numbers are not rational","B) Yes, because \u221211 = \u221211/1","C) No, only fractions are rational numbers","D) Yes, only if it can be expressed as a decimal"],correct_answer:"B) Yes, because \u221211 = \u221211/1",explanation:"Every integer is a rational number. \u221211 can be written as \u221211/1, where p = \u221211 and q = 1 are both integers and q \u2260 0. All integers (positive, negative, and zero) are rational numbers."},{id:"mcq03",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is the standard form of \u221218/24?",options:["A) \u221218/24","B) \u22123/4","C) 3/4","D) \u22129/12"],correct_answer:"B) \u22123/4",explanation:"To reduce \u221218/24: denominator 24 is positive \u2713. HCF(18,24) = 6. Divide both by 6: \u221218\xf76 = \u22123, 24\xf76 = 4. Standard form = \u22123/4. The denominator is positive and HCF(3,4) = 1, so \u22123/4 is in standard form."},{id:"mcq04",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following is equivalent to 3/5?",options:["A) 6/8","B) 9/15","C) 5/3","D) 4/7"],correct_answer:"B) 9/15",explanation:"To find equivalent rational numbers, multiply (or divide) both numerator and denominator by the same non-zero integer. 3/5 \xd7 3/3 = 9/15 \u2713. Check: 6/8 = 3/4 (not 3/5); 5/3 is the reciprocal; 4/7 is different."},{id:"mcq05",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Negative rational numbers are located _______ on the number line.",options:["A) To the right of zero","B) Between 0 and 1","C) To the left of zero","D) Above the number line"],correct_answer:"C) To the left of zero",explanation:"On the number line, positive numbers (including positive rational numbers) are to the right of zero, and negative numbers (including negative rational numbers) are to the left of zero. Zero itself is neither positive nor negative and is at the centre."},{id:"mcq06",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is the additive inverse of \u22124/7?",options:["A) 4/7","B) \u22127/4","C) 7/4","D) \u22124/7"],correct_answer:"A) 4/7",explanation:"The additive inverse of p/q is \u2212p/q. The additive inverse of \u22124/7 is \u2212(\u22124/7) = 4/7. Check: \u22124/7 + 4/7 = 0 \u2713. The additive inverse is the 'opposite' that gives 0 when added."},{id:"mcq07",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is the reciprocal of \u22125/8?",options:["A) 5/8","B) 8/5","C) \u22128/5","D) \u22125/8"],correct_answer:"C) \u22128/5",explanation:"The reciprocal of p/q is q/p \u2014 flip numerator and denominator. Reciprocal of \u22125/8 is 8/(\u22125) = \u22128/5. The sign stays the same. Check: (\u22125/8) \xd7 (\u22128/5) = 40/40 = 1 \u2713."},{id:"mcq08",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Calculate: 1/4 + 3/8.",options:["A) 4/12","B) 1/3","C) 5/8","D) 4/8"],correct_answer:"C) 5/8",explanation:"LCM(4,8) = 8. Convert 1/4 to 2/8. Then 2/8 + 3/8 = 5/8. Check: HCF(5,8)=1 and denominator positive, so 5/8 is already in standard form. Note: 4/12 = 1/3 is incorrect (adding numerators AND denominators \u2014 a common mistake)."},{id:"mcq09",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is (\u22122/3) \xd7 (\u22129/4)?",options:["A) \u22123/2","B) 18/12","C) 3/2","D) \u221218/12"],correct_answer:"C) 3/2",explanation:"Multiply numerators: (\u22122) \xd7 (\u22129) = 18 (negative \xd7 negative = positive). Multiply denominators: 3 \xd7 4 = 12. Result: 18/12 = 3/2 (dividing by HCF 6). The positive result comes from negative \xd7 negative = positive sign rule."},{id:"mcq10",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is (1/2) \xf7 (3/4)?",options:["A) 3/8","B) 2/3","C) 4/6","D) 1/6"],correct_answer:"B) 2/3",explanation:"Using KCF: Keep 1/2, Change \xf7 to \xd7, Flip 3/4 to 4/3. \u2192 (1/2) \xd7 (4/3) = 4/6 = 2/3. Note: 4/6 is not in standard form (HCF(4,6)=2), but 2/3 is. Option A (3/8) is wrong \u2014 it's the result of multiplying without flipping."},{id:"mcq11",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which of the following is NOT in standard form?",options:["A) \u22123/7","B) 5/9","C) \u22124/\u22126","D) \u22122/11"],correct_answer:"C) \u22124/\u22126",explanation:"Standard form requires: (1) positive denominator, (2) HCF(|numerator|, denominator) = 1. \u22124/\u22126 has a NEGATIVE denominator (\u22126), violating condition 1. It should be written as 4/6 \u2192 2/3 in standard form. The others all have positive denominators and are fully reduced."},{id:"mcq12",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Compare \u22123/4 and \u22125/8. Which is greater?",options:["A) \u22123/4","B) \u22125/8","C) They are equal","D) Cannot be determined"],correct_answer:"B) \u22125/8",explanation:"LCM(4,8) = 8. \u22123/4 = \u22126/8. Compare \u22126/8 and \u22125/8. Since \u22125 > \u22126 (less negative), \u22125/8 > \u22126/8. Therefore \u22125/8 > \u22123/4. With negative numbers: LESS negative = GREATER. \u22125/8 is closer to 0, so it is greater."},{id:"mcq13",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"A student adds 1/3 + 1/4 and gets 2/7. Is this correct?",options:["A) Yes, because 1+1=2 and 3+4=7","B) No, the correct answer is 7/12","C) No, the correct answer is 2/12","D) Yes, both methods give the same result"],correct_answer:"B) No, the correct answer is 7/12",explanation:"Adding numerators AND denominators is a common and WRONG method. The correct method: find LCM(3,4)=12. Convert: 1/3=4/12, 1/4=3/12. Add: 4/12+3/12=7/12. The answer is 7/12, not 2/7. Never add denominators!"},{id:"mcq14",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"How many rational numbers exist between 1/3 and 1/2?",options:["A) None","B) Exactly one","C) Exactly five","D) Infinitely many"],correct_answer:"D) Infinitely many",explanation:"Between any two distinct rational numbers, there are infinitely many rational numbers. This is the density property of rational numbers. Between 1/3 and 1/2, we can always find more: 5/12, 7/15, 9/20, etc. No matter how close two rational numbers are, infinitely many exist between them."},{id:"mcq15",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What is 0 divided by any non-zero rational number?",options:["A) 1","B) Undefined","C) 0","D) Infinity"],correct_answer:"C) 0",explanation:"0 divided by any non-zero number = 0. Using the formula: 0 \xf7 (r/s) = 0 \xd7 (s/r) = 0. This is different from dividing BY 0 (which is undefined). 0/5 = 0, 0/(\u22127/3) = 0. Zero divided by anything non-zero is always zero."},{id:"mcq16",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What is the result of (\u22127/5) + (7/5)?",options:["A) 14/5","B) 1","C) 0","D) \u22121"],correct_answer:"C) 0",explanation:"\u22127/5 and 7/5 are additive inverses of each other. Any number added to its additive inverse (opposite) gives 0. (\u22127/5) + (7/5) = (\u22127+7)/5 = 0/5 = 0. This is the definition of additive inverse."},{id:"mcq17",difficulty:"Hard",question:"Which of the following is the correct order from least to greatest: \u22122/3, 1/4, \u22121/2, 3/5?",options:["A) \u22122/3 < \u22121/2 < 1/4 < 3/5","B) \u22121/2 < \u22122/3 < 1/4 < 3/5","C) 3/5 < 1/4 < \u22121/2 < \u22122/3","D) \u22122/3 < 1/4 < \u22121/2 < 3/5"],correct_answer:"A) \u22122/3 < \u22121/2 < 1/4 < 3/5",explanation:"All negative rationals < 0 < all positive rationals. So the two negatives (\u22122/3, \u22121/2) come before the two positives (1/4, 3/5). Among negatives: LCM(3,2)=6. \u22122/3=\u22124/6, \u22121/2=\u22123/6. Since \u22124 < \u22123, \u22124/6 < \u22123/6, so \u22122/3 < \u22121/2. Among positives: LCM(4,5)=20. 1/4=5/20, 3/5=12/20. 5/20 < 12/20, so 1/4 < 3/5. Order: \u22122/3 < \u22121/2 < 1/4 < 3/5."},{id:"mcq18",difficulty:"Hard",question:"What is (\u22125/6) \u2212 (\u22127/9)?",options:["A) \u221212/18","B) \u22121/18","C) 1/18","D) 12/18"],correct_answer:"B) \u22121/18",explanation:"Subtracting a negative = adding its positive. (\u22125/6) \u2212 (\u22127/9) = (\u22125/6) + (7/9). LCM(6,9) = 18. Convert: \u22125/6 = \u221215/18; 7/9 = 14/18. Add: (\u221215 + 14)/18 = \u22121/18. Answer: \u22121/18."},{id:"mcq19",difficulty:"Hard",question:"If (p/q) \xd7 (\u22124/7) = 1, what is p/q?",options:["A) \u22127/4","B) 7/4","C) 4/7","D) \u22124/7"],correct_answer:"A) \u22127/4",explanation:"If the product of two numbers is 1, each is the reciprocal of the other. (p/q) \xd7 (\u22124/7) = 1 means p/q is the reciprocal (multiplicative inverse) of \u22124/7. Reciprocal of \u22124/7 = 7/(\u22124) = \u22127/4. Check: (\u22127/4) \xd7 (\u22124/7) = 28/28 = 1 \u2713."},{id:"mcq20",difficulty:"Hard",question:"A rational number is multiplied by its own additive inverse. What is the result?",options:["A) Always 0","B) Always 1","C) Always negative","D) Always positive or zero"],correct_answer:"C) Always negative",explanation:"The additive inverse of p/q is \u2212p/q. Multiplying: (p/q) \xd7 (\u2212p/q) = \u2212p\xb2/q\xb2. Since p\xb2 \u2265 0 and q\xb2 > 0, the result \u2212p\xb2/q\xb2 \u2264 0. If p \u2260 0, then \u2212p\xb2/q\xb2 < 0 (strictly negative). If p = 0, the number is 0 and its additive inverse is also 0, and 0 \xd7 0 = 0. So for any NON-ZERO rational number, multiplying by its additive inverse gives a NEGATIVE result. Answer C is most accurate for non-zero rationals."}]}},2778,[]);